{
  /*
   * L4 react — ES2023 plus React, and nothing else.
   *
   * "React Native is React, so the hooks travel" is the claim this whole package
   * rests on, and this is where the compiler is told to hold it. No DOM lib, so
   * `document`, `window` and `localStorage` are not names this directory can
   * spell. `"types": ["react"]` and nothing more, so `import.meta.env` does not
   * resolve either, and @types/node — which npm installs for the apps' config
   * files and which TypeScript would otherwise inject globally into every
   * project in the repo — cannot hand this layer `process`, `Buffer` or
   * `__dirname`.
   *
   * What `types` does NOT do, and it is worth knowing before trusting it: it
   * governs which @types packages are included AUTOMATICALLY, as globals. An
   * explicit `import { createPortal } from 'react-dom'` still resolves through
   * node_modules like any other package. Those are import edges, and import
   * edges are check-layers.mjs and check-platform.mjs territory — see the
   * DOM_MODULES list in the latter, which exists precisely because this config
   * cannot express that ban.
   *
   * The other soft edge: @types/react ships `global.d.ts`, which declares
   * `HTMLElement`, `KeyboardEvent` and ~150 more as EMPTY interfaces so that
   * React compiles under `--lib ES6` — that file is written for React Native. So
   * a bare `el: HTMLElement` annotation still type-checks here; `el.focus()`
   * does not, because the interface has no members. That gap is why
   * check-platform.mjs bans those names as identifiers rather than trusting the
   * lib setting alone.
   *
   * `"jsx": "react-jsx"` is inherited from the base. The JSX runtime types
   * resolve through @types/react; intrinsic elements like <div> resolve to those
   * empty stubs, which is correct enough — there is no <div> on React Native,
   * and this directory renders providers, not markup.
   *
   * ONE THING TO WATCH THAT WEB'S VERSION OF THIS FILE DID NOT HAVE TO: which
   * @types/react this project resolves. Hoisting puts web's (^19.2.x) and
   * mobile's (~19.1.x) in the tree at once, and web's tsconfig.app.json pins
   * itself to its own copy through `paths` for exactly that reason. This project
   * takes whatever the root resolves to, which is deliberate — the package must
   * compile against the OLDER of the two, since that is the floor React Native
   * sets. `kg/react/kg.tsx` uses React 19's provider-as-component form
   * (`<KgContext value={…}>`), which is why package.json declares react
   * >=19.1.0 rather than >=18.
   */
  "extends": "./tsconfig.base.json",
  "references": [{ "path": "./tsconfig.storage.json" }],
  "compilerOptions": {
    "tsBuildInfoFile": "./node_modules/.tmp/tsconfig.react.tsbuildinfo",
    "lib": ["ES2023"],
    "types": ["react"],
    /* Same reason as tsconfig.core.json: storage is a .d.ts here, never a source
       file, so `repo`'s driver choice cannot drag a platform up into the hooks. */
    "disableSourceOfProjectReferenceRedirect": true
  },
  "include": ["kg/react", "types/portable-globals.d.ts"]
}
